## 1. Operator philosophy

Twitter Advanced Search works with **boolean operators** that compose linearly into an X API v2 query string.

| UI field              | X API v2 operator              |
|-----------------------|-------------------------------|
| All of these words    | `keyword1 keyword2` (AND)     |
| This exact phrase     | `"exact phrase"`               |
| Any of these words    | `(word1 OR word2)`             |
| None of these words   | `-word`                        |
| Hashtags              | `#hashtag`                    |
| Language              | `lang:en`                     |
| From accounts         | `from:handle`                 |
| To accounts           | `to:handle`                   |
| Mentions              | `@handle`                     |
| Links only            | `has:links`                   |
| Specific URL/domain   | `url:example.com`             |
| Replies only          | `is:reply`                    |
| Exclude replies       | `-is:reply`                   |
| Exclude retweets      | `-is:retweet`                 |
| Media present         | `has:media` / `has:images`    |
| Native video          | `filter:native_video`         |
| Min likes             | `min_faves:100`               |
| Min retweets          | `min_retweets:50`             |
| Min replies           | `min_replies:10`              |
| Date since            | `since:2024-01-01`            |
| Date until            | `until:2024-03-31`            |
| Geo (place, slug/token) | `near:chicago within:2mi` or `near:"New York" within:10km` |
| Geo (coordinates)       | `geocode:40.7128,-74.0060,10mi` (radius mi/km) |

### Location operators & caveats (Bellingcat)

Guide: [Twitter Location Search](https://bellingcat.gitbook.io/toolkit/more/all-tools/twitter-location-search).

- Combine **`near:place`** with **`within:radius`** (`mi` or `km`), or use **`geocode:lat,lon,radius`** (e.g. `geocode:40.7128,-74.0060,10mi`).
- **Maximum geo radius:** X limits **`geocode:` / geo search radius to 25 miles**. Grond clamps larger radii in `clamp_within_radius_str` / `within_miles` / `within_km`.
- **Provenance is mixed:** results may reflect self-reported tweet location, profile location, or device GPS — treat matches as **noisy**; corroborate with independent sources (see `.cursor/rules/accuracy-patterns.mdc`).
- **Limited historical geo:** profile-driven georeference is often only reliable on recent windows (~last week in documented studies — see [Bellingcat geofenced COVID case study, May 2021](https://www.bellingcat.com/resources/2021/05/19/geofenced-searches-on-twitter-a-case-study-detailing-south-asias-covid-crisis/)).
- **Ethics / privacy:** minimize identifying detail in stored outputs; favour anonymization where appropriate; never substitute Twitter geo alone for ground truth.

Use `TwitterOsintTemplates.geo_event` or `location_crisis_monitor`; optional API fields `near_place`, `within_radius`, `geocode_lat`/`geocode_lon`/`geocode_radius` wire through `TwitterInput` → `_resolve_query`.

